===== README.md =====
# fixture

A fixture project.

[![npm](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/fixture.svg)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/fixture) [![CI](https://github.com/fixture/fixture/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/fixture/fixture/actions/workflows/ci.yml) [![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)

## Requirements

Node.js >= 24.0.0 (`.nvmrc` pins 24.18.0; run `nvm use`). Enforced via `engine-strict`, so installs fail fast on an unsupported version.

## Install

```sh
npm install fixture
```

## Usage

```jsx
import { Button } from 'fixture';

<Button label="Click me" />
```

## Releasing

Releases are handled by the `Release` workflow: push a changeset (`npx changeset`) and it opens a version PR, then publishes when that PR merges.

**Publishing needs npm credentials, which a new repository does not have.** Either:

- Set up [npm Trusted Publishing](https://docs.npmjs.com/trusted-publishers) (OIDC) — no secret to store or rotate, and the recommended option; or
- Add an [npm automation token](https://docs.npmjs.com/creating-and-viewing-access-tokens) as an `NPM_TOKEN` repository secret.

Until one of those is in place the `Release` workflow will fail with `ENEEDAUTH` on every push. That is expected on a brand-new repo.

## License

MIT © Fixture Author <fixture@example.com>

===== SECURITY.md =====
# Security Policy

If you discover a security vulnerability, please **do not** open a public issue.
Instead, report it privately to the maintainers (e.g. via GitHub Security
Advisories). We will respond as quickly as possible.

